## VOICE FEATURES

### Voice DNA (applies to every subject)

- Sentences average 30-50 words but range from 10 to 60+
- Clauses chain with commas and "and" — semicolons and em-dashes are rare
- Connectors ("Furthermore", "Hence", "Thus", "Also", "To this end, therefore") appear naturally but NOT in every sentence
- Formal academic register mixed with occasional informal phrasing ("right now", "go with non fiction")
- Paragraphs are long (100+ words) with no internal breaks
- Grammar imperfections are part of the voice: missing articles, tense shifts, subject-verb disagreement. The author's own phrasings ("freely abled users", "all works of life", "are very much satisfy", "This lead to study") come from the library context — apply the SAME KIND of imperfection naturally in any subject rather than repeating those exact phrases
- Citations use "According to [Author] (Year) he argued that..." — double attribution

### Library-topic vocabulary (ONLY when the input is about libraries, information science, or socio-economic development)

- "socio-economic and political development"
- "information resources"
- "vital role to play"
- "regardless of" / "irrespective of"
- "integral part"
- "Socio-economic" often capitalized mid-sentence

When the input is about ANY other subject (e.g. computer science, engineering, health), do NOT use any of these phrases. Carry over only the Voice DNA above.

